---
import SiteLayout from '../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { getExperience, experienceFrame } from '../../../data/experiences';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/paths';
import Term from '../../../components/Term.astro';
import { RUN } from '../../../lessons/instrument-flight/ils-trainer-model.mjs';
import { levelTrim } from '../../../lessons/instrument-flight/attitude-model.mjs';
import '../../../styles/instrument-flight.css';
const exercise = getExperience('instrument-trainer');
const mod = exercise.module;
const trainerHref = sitePath(experienceFrame(exercise));
const attitude = sitePath('manual/instrument-flight/attitude-instrument-flying/');
const procedures = sitePath('manual/instrument-flight/instrument-procedures/');
const core = [
 ['example', 'One run, start to finish'], ['intercept', 'Intercept and the wind'], ['tracking', 'Tracking both needles'],
 ['decision', 'Limits and the decision at the DA'], ['recap', 'Key points'], ['launch', 'Practice: the hand-flown ILS'],
];
const reference = [['model', 'What the exercise models'], ['sims', 'In the sim'], ['check', 'Check your understanding'], ['sources', 'Sources and applicability']];
// [1] AFMAN 11-217 Vol 1 (2010) by printed page; [2] CNATRA P-1204 by
// printed page, for the needle scaling and the ILS technique only.
const pp = (n: number) => (page: string) => `[${n}, ${/[–,]/.test(page) ? 'pp.' : 'p.'} ${page}]`;
const af = pp(1), t45 = pp(2);
const da = RUN.tdze + RUN.da, locMins = RUN.tdze + RUN.locMins;
const descent = levelTrim(RUN.speed, { dragK: RUN.dragK, gamma: -3 }), level = levelTrim(RUN.speed, { dragK: RUN.dragK });
const r0 = (v: number) => Math.round(v);
---
<SiteLayout title={mod.title} activeSection="manual" activeSubject="instrument-flight" compactSubjectNav={true}
 breadcrumbs={[{label:'Manual',href:sitePath('manual/')},{label:'Instrument Flight',href:sitePath('manual/instrument-flight/')},{label:mod.title}]}>
 <section class="status-panel">
  <h2>{mod.maturity}</h2>
  <p>Module revision {mod.revision} · Practice for instrument flying with nothing outside the window: a generic panel, an invented aircraft, and only the instruments to go on. Its first exercise is the hand-flown ILS, a run of about 90 seconds from the localizer intercept to the decision altitude, with a new wind and start each time. Written for military pilots of every service, from the Air Force's instrument flight procedures manual.</p>
  <p>Read <a href={attitude}>Attitude Instrument Flying</a> for the crosscheck and <a href={`${procedures}#ils`}>Flying the ILS</a> for the approach first. The exercise opens in its own window.</p>
 </section>
 <nav class="contents" aria-labelledby="contents-title"><h2 id="contents-title">In this module</h2>
  <div class="contents-groups">
   <div><h3>The core sequence</h3><ol>{core.map(([id,label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol></div>
   <div><h3>Reference</h3><ol>{reference.map(([id,label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol></div>
  </div>
 </nav>

 <p class="lede">An ILS flown by hand is the crosscheck under pressure. The needles are angles, so they grow more sensitive all the way down, and the wind you were not told about pushes you off the localizer from the moment you intercept it. At the end you have a few seconds to decide whether to land or go missed. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210')}</a> <a href="#source-t45">{t45('1-2')}</a></p>
 <p>By the end you should be able to:</p>
 <ol class="outcomes">
  <li id="o1">intercept the localizer and find the heading that holds it in an unknown wind;</li>
  <li id="o2">capture the glide slope and hold both needles within one dot with small attitude and power changes;</li>
  <li id="o3">recognise the needle limits that end an approach, and go missed when one is broken;</li>
  <li id="o4">make the land or go-missed decision at the DA from what you can see and where the needles are.</li>
 </ol>

 <h2 id="example">One run, start to finish</h2>
 <p>An invented run, as the exercise flies it. Final course 090, DA {da} ft ({RUN.da} ft above the touchdown zone), {RUN.speed} knots, gear and flaps down.</p>
 <ol class="exchange">
  <li><span class="who">Start</span><span class="said">Level at 1,200 ft above the field, 4 NM out, heading 065: the course bar is one dot right and the glide slope pointer one dot above centre, so you are left of course and below the glide slope. <a href="#intercept" class="step">Intercept</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Intercept</span><span class="said">The bar starts to move. Roll out on 090 as it centres. It drifts left again: a crosswind from the left. Turn 5° left, to 085, and the bar stops. That is the heading that holds the course. <a href="#intercept" class="step">The wind</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Glide slope</span><span class="said">The pointer comes down to the centre. Lower the nose about 3° and take off a little power, and the VSI settles near 700 ft a minute. <a href="#tracking" class="step">Tracking</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Final</span><span class="said">Half a dot above the glide slope (the pointer half a dot low): ease the nose down a degree, then put it back as the pointer returns. Bar a quarter dot right: 2° right, then back. Each correction smaller than the last as you get closer. <a href="#tracking" class="step">Tracking</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">DA</span><span class="said">Approach lights in sight, both needles inside one dot: land. Still in cloud, or more than a dot off: go missed. <a href="#decision" class="step">Decision</a></span></li>
 </ol>

 <h2 id="intercept">Intercept and the wind</h2>
 <p>Before the localizer, have the ILS tuned and identified and the front course set; before glide slope intercept, be configured and on speed with the landing checklist done. On the localizer, correct with heading changes of no more than 5° either side of the wind-corrected heading. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210')}</a> <a href="#source-t45">{t45('6-60, 6-62')}</a></p>
 <p>The wind-corrected heading is the one that keeps the course bar still, not centred: find it by flying the course heading, watching which way the bar drifts, and taking a few degrees into the wind until it stops. Then centre the bar with a small turn and come back to the corrected heading. For the drift angle in degrees, divide the crosswind by your true airspeed in miles a minute: 12 knots of crosswind at 140 knots (about 2.3 NM a minute) is about 5°. <a href="#source-afman">{af('157–158')}</a></p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> hold a heading that stops the bar moving, then make small corrections either side of it.</p>

 <h2 id="tracking">Tracking both needles</h2>
 <p>Fly toward the needles: course bar right, the course is to your right; glide slope pointer high, the glide slope is above you. Full deflection is two dots, about 2.5° either side of the localizer course and 0.7° above or below the glide slope. <a href="#source-t45">{t45('1-2')}</a> <a href="#source-afman">{af('210')}</a> Because they are angles, one dot is over 700 ft sideways at 4 NM but under 300 ft at the DA in the exercise's geometry, so a correction that was right far out is too big near minimums.</p>
 <p>As the glide slope pointer centres, set the descent attitude and power to stay on speed and on the glidepath. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210')}</a> <a href="#source-t45">{t45('6-62')}</a> For a 3° glidepath the vertical speed is about ground speed × 10 ÷ 2: at 140 knots of ground speed, 700 ft a minute; more with a tailwind, less into a headwind. <a href="#source-afman">{af('208')}</a> Make each change on the attitude indicator first, then let the performance instruments show whether it was enough, as in <a href={attitude}>Attitude Instrument Flying</a>.</p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> small attitude changes, held, then checked; and smaller ones the closer you get.</p>

 <h2 id="decision">Limits and the decision at the DA</h2>
 <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>When an ILS ends <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210, 250')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Rule</th><th>What you do</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><td>Anywhere on final</td><td>Full-scale localizer deflection, or an unreliable localizer</td><td>Discontinue the approach</td></tr>
   <tr><td>Below localizer minimums</td><td>More than one dot (half scale) below, or two dots (full scale) above, the glide slope</td><td>Do not descend below localizer minimums; recapture within limits and you may continue to the DA</td></tr>
   <tr><td>At the DA</td><td>Runway environment in sight and in position to land safely</td><td>Continue to land</td></tr>
   <tr><td>At the DA</td><td>No required visual references</td><td>Go missed, immediately</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>The runway environment is any one of the approach lights, threshold, runway end identifier lights, touchdown zone, runway or its markings and lights, or the visual approach slope indicator; the full list, with its approach-light limit, is in <a href={`${procedures}#approach`}>The approach, down to minimums</a>. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209')}</a> "In position to land safely" has no number in the source. The exercise draws the line at one dot on both needles, which is its own rule.</p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> at the DA there are only two answers, and the default is the missed approach.</p>

 <h2 id="recap">Key points</h2>
 <ul class="recap">
  <li>Find the heading that stops the course bar moving, and correct no more than 5° either side of it.</li>
  <li>The needles are angles: the same correction moves them further the closer you are.</li>
  <li>Full-scale localizer on final means discontinue; below localizer minimums, stay within one dot low and two dots high.</li>
  <li>At the DA, land only with the runway environment in sight and in position to land; otherwise go missed at once.</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="launch">Practice: the hand-flown ILS</h2>
 <section class="status-panel" data-experience-id={exercise.id} data-experience-revision={exercise.revision}>
  <h3>{exercise.title}</h3><p>{exercise.maturity} · Exercise revision {exercise.revision}</p>
  <p>{exercise.applicability} Prerequisites: {exercise.prerequisites.join(' · ')}.</p>
  <p>Each run starts level below the glide slope, off the localizer, on an intercept heading, with a random wind you are not told. Fly both needles to the DA on the panel from <a href={attitude}>Attitude Instrument Flying</a>, then land or go missed. The debrief scores the time within one dot, the worst deviations, any limit broken and the decision, and replays the flown path on a plan and profile view beside the panel.</p>
  <p>Controls: the arrow keys move the attitude (<kbd>↑</kbd> nose down, <kbd>↓</kbd> nose up, <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> bank), <kbd>W</kbd> and <kbd>S</kbd> the power, <kbd>G</kbd> goes missed and <kbd>L</kbd> lands at the DA. An optional <strong>Auto power</strong> switch holds the speed for you, so you can learn to fly the glide slope with pitch alone before adding the power. A joystick or HOTAS can be assigned to the stick and throttle, and a game controller works with its left stick and triggers, and on a phone or with a mouse there are buttons under the panel. No joystick is needed.</p>
  <p class="launch"><a class="launch-button" href={trainerHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-experience-launch data-experience-id={exercise.id}>Launch the instrument trainer<span> (opens in a new window)</span></a></p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="model">What the exercise models</h2>
 <p>The needles use the same scaling as <a href={`${procedures}#ils`}>Figure 2 of Instrument Procedures</a>: 2.5° and 0.7° full scale, two dots each way, a 3° glide slope. The limits and the decision follow the Air Force manual. <a href="#source-afman">{af('209–210')}</a> <a href="#source-t45">{t45('1-2')}</a></p>
 <p>Everything else is invented: the aircraft, a generic jet that holds the attitude you set and needs about {r0(level.power)}% power level and {r0(descent.power)}% on the glide slope at {RUN.speed} knots, gear and flaps down; the field elevation of {RUN.tdze} ft; the DA of {da} ft ({RUN.da} ft above the touchdown zone) and localizer minimums of {locMins} ft; winds up to {RUN.windKt[1]} knots from any direction; the runway environment being in sight at the DA on about {r0(RUN.visualChance * 100)}% of runs; and "in position to land" meaning within one dot on both needles. True airspeed is taken as indicated, there is no turbulence, and the run pauses at the DA for the decision, which in the aircraft is immediate, then ends there: the landing and the missed approach itself are not flown.</p>
 <p class="note">The trainer will gain more exercises on the same panel: basic instrument work, crosscheck traps, unusual attitude recovery and holding.</p>

 <section class="sim-panel" id="sims">
  <h2>In the sim</h2>
  <p>How to tune and fly an ILS in DCS, and where the A-10C II manual's glide slope rule differs from the Air Force manual's, is in <a href={`${procedures}#sims`}>Instrument Procedures, In the sim</a>. The exercise practises the part every sim shares: flying the needles by hand to the DA. No sim manual was read for this page.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">1</span>You fly the final course heading and the course bar drifts steadily right. Where is the wind from, and what do you do?</summary><p>From the right. Turn a few degrees right of the course to find the heading that stops the bar, centre it with a small turn, then hold the corrected heading.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">2</span>Half a dot above the glide slope at 4 NM took a 1° nose-down change. Why is the same change too much at 1 NM?</summary><p>The needles are angles: a dot is far fewer feet close in, so the same change moves the pointer much further and faster. Make smaller, quicker corrections near minimums.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">3</span>Below localizer minimums the glide slope pointer sits one and a half dots above centre: the glide slope is above you. May you keep descending?</summary><p>No. You are more than one dot low; do not descend below localizer minimums until you are back within limits.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">3</span>The course bar pegs at full scale on final. What now?</summary><p>Discontinue the approach and go missed.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">4</span>At the DA the approach lights are in sight, but the course bar is a dot and a half right. Land?</summary><p>In the exercise, no: it counts as not in position to land. In real flying the source asks for a position from which a safe landing is possible, which a dot and a half off near the DA is unlikely to give.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <p id="source-afman"><strong>[1] US Air Force, AFMAN 11-217 Volume 1, Instrument Flight Procedures, 22 October 2010.</strong> <a href="https://www.code7700.com/pdfs/usaf/afman11-217v1.pdf">Read on code7700.com's copy</a>, 2 November 2026, by printed page: holding drift (pp. 157–158), final approach descent rate (p. 208), below minimums and the ILS (pp. 209–210), missed approach (p. 250). Superseded by the consolidated AFMAN 11-217 of 10 June 2019, which could not be retrieved; numbers may have changed.</p>
 <p id="source-t45"><strong>[2] Chief of Naval Air Training, CNATRA P-1204 (Rev. 09-16, Change 2 of 25 March 2021), Flight Training Instruction, Instrument, T-45C MPTS and IUT.</strong> <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20211009235549/https://www.cnatra.navy.mil/local/docs/pat-pubs/P-1204.pdf">Internet Archive copy of cnatra.navy.mil</a> (October 2021 snapshot), read 2 November 2026 by printed page: ILS needle scaling (p. 1-2), configuring before glide slope intercept (p. 6-60) and localizer corrections (p. 6-62). A training command instruction for one aircraft type, cited for these generic points only.</p>
 <p>Page excerpts are in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/instrument-flight-evidence.md">Instrument flight evidence</a>.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: generic instrument procedures for fixed-wing pilots of every service. Your service's instrument manual, the aircraft's flight manual and the published approach govern real approaches, their minimums and their limits. The exercise's aircraft and approach are invented. This revision is Drafted pending independent source-backed review.</p>
</SiteLayout>
